al). Weaknesses: regarded as pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone (communication, crew cooperation). But these are interpersonal.\n\nCandidate C strengths: 100% reliable, makes correct decisions quickly (decision quality), puts concern for others above everything (crew cooperation), excellent attention skills (attention), can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality). Weaknesses: difficulty communicating ideas (communication), egocentric (crew cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So both have communication/crew cooperation issues.\n\nCandidate D strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), concentration, problem solving, responsibility. Weaknesses: moody, loner (crew cooperation), weak leadership.\n\nCandidate A strengths: anticipates dangerous situations (decision quality), sees complex connections (technical competence), spatial vision (technical), leadership. Weaknesses: unfriendly (crew cooperation), eats unhealthily (maybe health, not criteria). Also no mention of reliability or stress.\n\nThus top two are B and C. Need to decide which overall fit.\n\nConsider criteria weighting: reliability is critical; both B and C have strong reliability (B: very conscientious, C: 100% reliable). Stress resilience: B handles stress very well; C no explicit stress resilience fact. Technical competence: B good at assessing weather, computer skills; C not mentioned technical competence beyond attention. Decision quality: C makes correct decisions quickly; B not mentioned decision speed. Crew cooperation: C puts concern for others above everything (positive), but also egocentric and communication difficulty; B pretentious, wrong tone. Professional communication: B sometimes wrong tone; C difficulty communicating ideas. Adaptability: B no note about willingness to education; C not willing to further education (negative). Responsibility: B not mentioned; C not mentioned; B maybe implied by conscientiousness. D has responsibility.\n\nThus trade-offs: B strong on stress, technical, reliability; C strong on decision quality, attention, reliability, crew concern. Communication issues for both.\n\nWhich is stronger overall? Might argue B is more balanced across core safety pillars (reliability, stress, technical). C's decision speed is important but not enough if communication issues hamper crew. Also C's low adaptability is concerning for long-distance flights where ongoing learning needed.\n\nThus I might shift recommendation to B, aligning with Markus, but need to provide reasoning and reference both contributions.\n\nAlternatively, could propose D? But D lacks reliability evidence, and crew cooperation issues.\n\nThus I will support Candidate B as the best overall fit, citing explicit facts, and note that while B has interpersonal issues, they are manageable.\n\nWe must produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate B\".\n\nWe should reference prior discussion: agree with Markus that B's strengths outweigh C's weaknesses, and note that C's communication and adaptability concerns are serious.\n\nWe could also ask for clarification about C's crew cooperation: maybe ask another panel member about evidence of positive crew atmosphere? But we have note \"puts concern for others above everything\" which suggests crew cooperation. But we could ask for more detail on C's communication difficulty severity. However we can proceed without tool.\n\nThus produce final answer."}
